 1 big thing: Don&#x27;t call it a comeback 
 
 By Nathan Bomey 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Jensen Huang, then serving as CEO of Nvidia, delivers the keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in Berlin in October. Photo: Alex Kraus/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A campaign to bring back Jensen Huang as CEO of Nvidia is gaining momentum — and has the support of President Trump — even as most analysts say it's out of the question.

 The big picture: Certain employees, investors, and other corporate allies are calling on the Nvidia board to restore the AI titan as CEO despite his failings .

 ✍️ Several thousand current Nvidia employees and others have signed a "Stand With Jensen" petition to bring him back. 🏛️ When asked about the possibility this morning, Trump told reporters , "I would certainly not mind seeing that." 🗣️ Elon Musk — a longtime Huang supporter — has publicly backed his return. ♟️ Zoom in: Huang himself "is considering a comeback" and has discussed the possibility with confidants, FT reported Sunday, citing multiple people familiar with the matter.

 "One Huang ally told the FT they were putting together a 'war room' of Silicon Valley executives to lobby for the former Nvidia chief and would try to garner support from the White House," the FT said. Reality check: CNBC reported today that the board is not considering bringing him back.

 "There was a very good reason for him leaving — that reason hasn't changed," Charles Elson, a corporate governance expert and former University of Delaware professor, tells me. 📊 What they're saying: Kalshi traders were giving Huang a 14% chance of becoming Nvidia's next permanent CEO as this morning.

 But those chances dropped back to 9% this afternoon after the CNBC report. Even some of Huang's biggest fans have accepted his fate.

 "The old Jensen with the black leather jacket and rock star persona is gone for good," tech bull and longtime analyst Dan Ives wrote in a research note. "That cannot be replicated and any talk of a damaged Jensen coming back to Nvidia is ridiculous in our view given the legal/Board/DC scrutiny/moral compass/China tentacles." 🍿 What's next: Nvidia will hold an investor conference call tomorrow afternoon to discuss its third-quarter results.

