 2. Closing the China chip loophole 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Whoever is in charge of Nvidia in the long term may find it harder to sell chips to certain customers.

 The Trump administration today moved to close a loophole that has given Chinese companies backdoor access to American AI computing power. 🇨🇳 How it works: U.S. export controls restrict the sale of certain advanced AI chips to China — but Chinese companies have accessed those chips by renting computing capacity from data centers in other countries.

 The rule targets data centers abroad that supply "significant volumes of computing power" to companies in high risk countries, including China, Russia and Iran. The data centers have to vet their customers. The big picture The action to close the loophole comes after mounting pressure on the Commerce Department to block a top U.S. adversary from accessing key technology in the AI race, Axios' Maria Curi writes .

 Huang's affair with an Nvidia customer and alleged Chinese spy tied to Megaspeed — a data center company in Singapore — have intensified calls to tackle the diversion of chips to China. 🥊 The intrigue : The interim final rule was released by Commerce but "written by and at the direction of the Department of Treasury and the National Security Council," a press release states. 

 That language appears to nod to an ongoing and longstanding feud between Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick and other top administration officials. What we're watching: One of those officials is Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent, who took a shot at Huang yesterday, calling on Nvidia's board to select an outside CEO.

